# Movable ghost camera for framing shots

Status: IN_PROGRESS
Milestone: A viewpoint that can be moved independently of the capture camera
Dependencies: 12_PERSPECTIVE_LAB.todo, 13_BLENDER_MODEL_RUNTIME.todo, 28_STAGE_BASE_DISTANCE.todo

## Purpose
The capture camera decides what the performer is doing. There is currently no way to decide where
that performance is watched from, because the renderer has no camera at all: vertices arrive in
pixel coordinates, placement is a scale and a translation, and the room is a cached background.

Add a free-fly ghost camera so shots can be framed from any position, following replay-system
practice where the camera is a view over recorded state and never part of it. Capture, tracking
and the recorded observations must be completely unaffected by where the camera is.

## Tasks
- [x] Research replay and spectator camera systems against primary engine source.
- [x] Add camera state, named modes, noclip movement, mouse look and framing helpers.
- [x] Add view and projection matrices for the project's existing coordinate convention.
- [x] Cover frame-rate independence, clamping and validation with tests.
- [x] Document the design, the borrowed principles and the honest limits.
- [x] Project world geometry to pixels so the existing rasteriser needs no shader change.
- [x] Draw the perspective lab through the movable camera, reusing its world surfaces.
- [x] Render an imported rig from an arbitrary viewpoint.
- [x] Prove it end to end: one pose, six angles, every shot distinct and populated.
- [ ] Add the Studio control, pointer capture and key bindings.
- [ ] Wire a subject to orbit and attached modes.
- [ ] Split triangles against the near plane instead of dropping them.
- [ ] Give the built-in character a camera path, or document it as imported-only.
- [ ] Add stored shots, and decide whether cinematic paths belong in this workstream.
- [ ] Produce recorded-source evidence from several angles.

## Acceptance criteria
- [x] The camera holds no observation, asset or runtime state.
- [x] Moving the camera cannot alter tracking, timestamps, availability or diagnostics.
- [x] Pitch is clamped short of the pole so yaw stays recoverable.
- [x] Movement is frame-rate independent and mouse look is not scaled by frame time.
- [x] A long frame cannot reverse or teleport the camera.
- [x] Moving the camera visibly changes the rendered viewpoint.
- [x] The character and the room are drawn through the same camera.
- [x] Looking away from the room does not raise or tear.
- [x] Off-axis views are labelled as monocular estimates rather than measured geometry.
- [ ] The viewpoint can be moved from the application rather than from a tool.

## Completion evidence
- src\trickroom\ghostcam.py adds GhostCamera, CameraCommand, named modes, basis, view_matrix,
  perspective, project_to_screen, framing_distance and dolly_zoom.
- src\trickroom\room.py adds CameraProjection and camera_room, reusing the existing world-space
  surface definitions. The fixed stage projection is unchanged and still cached.
- src\trickroom\assets\presentation.py adds camera_batches for the imported rig.
- tools\check_ghost_camera.py renders one identical pose from six positions.
- Full suite 493 tests, no failures, errors or skips. Ruff check and format pass.
  tools\check_docs.py exits 0.
- End-to-end run: front, three-quarter, side, behind, low and high all drew all 606 triangles,
  every pair of images differs, and passed is true with exit 0. Subject pixels vary with angle as
  a human silhouette should: 16070 from the side against 33975 from behind. The high shot measures
  233px tall against about 394px for the level shots, which is the expected foreshortening.
- No regression: tools\check_studio.py still passes 19 native groups with no callback errors, and
  a CLI demo render still completes with exit 0.
- Two bugs were found and fixed during validation rather than being written around. Position was
  integrated by the end-of-step velocity, which left movement frame-rate dependent by about three
  percent between 15 and 120fps; it now uses the analytic integral of the exponential approach.
  Two test expectations were also wrong: wrap_yaw(540) is -180 in a half-open range, and a dolly
  zoom moving closer must widen the angle, not narrow it.
- Limits: there is no Studio control yet, so this is reachable from the tool and not the
  application. Only imported rigs can be viewed off-axis; the built-in character is posed from
  image-space landmarks and has no real depth. Near-plane triangles are dropped, not clipped.
  Nothing here improves a monocular pose estimate, and steep angles will expose depth error that a
  front view hides.

## Completion log
- 2026-10-16 - Projection path, camera-driven room, imported-rig rendering and the six-angle
  end-to-end check completed and validated. Studio control and live input remain.
- 2026-10-14 - Requested by the owner. Researched against Quake and Source SDK source, implemented
